        ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{7}y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{7}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{7}y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+{x}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)+{x}^{7}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{y}+{x}^{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,1/2)} & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+{1}^{7} \\\\  & =2e+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
