        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-159607",
        ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y+{x}^{4}y,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y+{x}^{4}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡y)+{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)+{x}^{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sec}⁡y\\operatorname{tan}⁡y+{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1+4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
