        ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)-x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)-x\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,\\pi /2)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
