        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-159609",
        ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2{x}^{5}{e}^{y},$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2{x}^{5}{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{5}{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2{e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-2{e}^{y}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}-10{x}^{4}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{1}-10\\cdot {1}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-30\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      }
